print_objects_help() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Usage:
  sixb objects inspect <object-type> <primary-id> [--depth <0-3>] [--max-objects <1-100>] [--full]
  sixb objects list [options]
  sixb objects get <object-type> <primary-id>...
  sixb objects search <text> [--limit <n>]
  sixb objects query --file <path|-> [--include-total|--no-total]
  sixb objects query --example <name>
  sixb objects count --file <path|->
  sixb objects exists --file <path|->
  sixb objects facets --file <path|->
  sixb objects links <object-type> <primary-id> [options]

List options:
  --type <id>                         Exact ontology object type id
  --limit <n>                         0 through 1000
  --offset <n>
  --order-by createdAt|updatedAt|primaryId
  --order asc|desc
  --id-prefix <value>                 Primary-id prefix
  --id-suffix <value>                 Primary-id suffix
  --created-after|--created-before <RFC3339>
  --updated-after|--updated-before <RFC3339>

Links options:
  --link <link-id>
  --direction outgoing|incoming|both  Defaults to both
  --page-size <1-1000>                Defaults to 1000
  --page-token <token>                Continue an edge page
  --include-objects                   Include selected and current-page endpoint objects

Use `objects inspect` first when the user or app context identifies an object. It follows both
relationship directions to depth 2 by default, hydrates up to 40 objects, and returns a bounded
graph with the actual persisted relationships. Use `--depth 0` for only the object.

Inspect omits materialization timestamps and ontology definitions by default. Use `--full` when
storage timestamps, declared links, or available actions are needed.

`objects get` uses a refs query without ontology discovery or identity URL paths. It is safe for
opaque ids containing :, /, #, ?, or %. `objects query` accepts either a query node or a full
{query, includeTotal} envelope. All identifiers are case-sensitive and come from `ontology get`.
EOF
}

print_query_help() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Usage:
  sixb objects query --file <path|-> [--include-total|--no-total]
  sixb objects query --example <exact|filter|incoming|expand|sort|page|facets>
  sixb objects query --example list

Input is a query node. A full {"query": ...} request is also accepted. The CLI supplies
includeTotal unless the full request already contains it.

Query nodes compose through `input`:
  start     {"kind":"start","objectTypeId":"Type","includeSubtypes":false}
  refs      {"kind":"refs","refs":[{"objectTypeId":"Type","primaryId":"opaque:id"}]}
  filter    {"kind":"filter","input":<query>,"predicate":<predicate>}
  text      {"kind":"text","input":<query>,"query":"words","fields":["name"]}
  traverse  {"kind":"traverse","input":<query>,"linkId":"link","direction":"outgoing"}
  vector    {"kind":"vector","input":<query>,"propertyId":"embedding","vector":[0.1],"k":10}
  set       {"kind":"set","op":"union|intersect|subtract","inputs":[<query>,<query>]}
  sort      {"kind":"sort","input":<query>,"fields":[<sort-field>]}
  limit     {"kind":"limit","input":<query>,"limit":20}
  page      {"kind":"page","input":<query>,"pageSize":20,"pageToken":"optional"}
  project   {"kind":"project","input":<query>,"properties":["id","name"]}
  expand    {"kind":"expand","input":<query>,"expansions":[<expansion>]}

Predicates use `op`, not `kind`:
  and/or    {"op":"and","items":[<predicate>,<predicate>]}
  not       {"op":"not","item":<predicate>}
  eq/neq    {"op":"eq","propertyId":"status","value":"open"}
  lt/lte/gt/gte
  in        {"op":"in","propertyId":"status","values":["open","closed"]}
  exists    {"op":"exists","propertyId":"owner","value":true}
  contains  {"op":"contains","propertyId":"name","value":"sixb"}

Sort fields:
  {"kind":"property","propertyId":"name","direction":"asc|desc"}
  {"kind":"relevance","direction":"asc|desc"}

Traversal and expansion directions are outgoing or incoming. For incoming relationships, the link
is declared on the child/source type; add `sourceObjectTypeId` when needed to disambiguate it.
Expansion items accept linkId, direction, sourceObjectTypeId, limit, orderBy, and nested expand.

Rules:
  - Run `sixb ontology get <type>` first; never guess property or link ids.
  - Use refs for exact identities; it does not require ontology metadata.
  - Other filters require query.searchable:true and query.filterable:true.
  - Property sorting requires query.sortable:true. Top-level object timestamps are list options,
    not ontology-property sorts unless the ontology separately declares those properties.
  - Put limits and pages inside the query tree, not at the HTTP request top level.
  - Use `sixb objects facets --file ...` for {query, facets} documents.
EOF
}

